excess.evidence:
Core never asks for either section. `git grep -n 'fetch_snapshot' -- core/lib` gives exactly two call sites, rails_bootstrap.rb:118 `sections: %w[routes]` and server.rb:3426 `sections: ["routes"]`; AgentProcessManager#fetch_snapshot's `sections:` is a required keyword, so the `|| ["metadata"]` default at agent.rb:112 is unreachable from the wire too. The only requests for `sections: ["models"]` in the tree are agent_spec.rb:303, :475, :495 — the section's own unit spec.

The "models" section was superseded by agent/models in 008.5; 05-protocol.md:121 documents it as the lighter alternative, and rails_bootstrap.rb:108-113 records why the bulk call replaced it.

@generation's only exposure is #metadata_section:513 and agent/reload's reply. Nothing reads the reply: `git grep -n 'changedSections\|\[:generation' -- core/lib` returns only the two definitions and two comments, and all three reload call sites (server.rb:3424, :3504, :3609) test the result for truthiness alone.

The same three-line give-up transition is written twice inside one class:

  @active = false; @gave_up = true; @epoch += 1

Both sites carry a comment giving the same reason for the epoch bump ("Moves the epoch for the same reason #reset does: a fetch that was already in flight ... would otherwise land"). A future change to what giving up means has to find both.

The body is one line and uses only `instance_entries`:

  def rooted_instance_chain?(type, instance_entries, _singleton)
    instance_entries.any? { |e| e.identified? && Index::SymbolId.qualify_owner(e.name) == "::BasicObject" }
  end

Both `type` and `_singleton` are unused. They are the leavings of the 0.2.10 module-exception attempt that the comment directly above (:243-261) records as rolled back after a drive round measured '41 findings added, 0 removed, and every one of the 41 false'. CLAUDE.md names this exact hazard — 'A revert is the change most likely to leave documentation behind' — and 024.47's inventory is the precedent; here it is code the revert left rather than prose.

minimum:
For each reference candidate the parser already produced, decide whether the engine can *prove* a claim, and only then emit one. That needs, once each:

1. one receiver type per candidate, taken from the layer that already produces receiver types (`Semantic::ReceiverResolution`), with the three "decline rather than assert" rules that 024.47 established belong *here* rather than in resolution;
2. one enumerability answer per receiver, taken from the layer that enumerates members (`MethodResolver#availability`), plus the one question that layer cannot ask — the Runtime Agent round trip;
3. one signature/declaration lookup per call, shared by the "does anything declare this" and "what are this call's parameters" questions (`declared_signature_for`);
4. an output value carrying exactly what its two readers consume: `Server#to_lsp_diagnostic` reads range/severity/code/message/related_information, and `Server#publish_findings` reads generation. Nothing else.

Everything in items 1-3 is present and is, as far as I could measure, the minimum. The excess is concentrated in item 4 and in machinery that no shipped caller reaches.
